  "notes": "Flyte Tyme's funk-and-synth production board; the research windows them **1990–2001** for Gate 2, excluding their 80s Time/Janet origin era, and that window is used verbatim. Tempo is \"84–110 straight, plus ballads 60–75 half-time\", with the band edges marked `[LOW-CONF]`; the straight band is the base, mode 96. Three drum deltas from the entry: \"early and heavy adopters of the **Roland TR-808** — the 808 kick and snare are their signature layer\", so `lockTo808` is 0.5 and the snare is a stack; \"programmed with the NJS swung-16th feel through the early 90s\" gives `swing.amount` 0.58 with real ghost activity; and \"percussion is stacked in layers (shaker, tambourine, timbale hits) rather than left bare\" gives the five-lane perc bed at three to six hits a bar, which is the widest lane set among the producers here and the parameter that separates them from every other 808 adopter in this section. The identifying claim is the bass: \"synth bass playing a *riff*, not just roots — the bassline is a compositional element, doubled by keys\", and their documented approach \"fuses the rhythmic elements of funk and disco with heavy doses of synthesizers\". So `followRootsProb` drops to 0.4, the passing-tone set is six wide, `anticipationProb` is 0.44 and the cells are funk shapes. Harmony is \"keyboard/piano/synth-led 4-chord cycles with 7ths and 9ths, a synth-brass or bell hook\", the hook itself being the counter at a 0.5 density; \"they build around the singer's speaking register\" is the low, narrow melody register. ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: the 808 lineage and stylistic description are sourced but \"specific tempo/harmonic parameters are board-level inference\", and the key claim carries a `[LOW-CONF]` mark. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"minor-mode funk vamps with major-mode bridges [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
